The Stranger's storm, redrawn: mockup round 2 (waiting for .go before wiring)

Branch stills/stranger-storm-art, folder stranger-storm-art/round2/ (round 1 stays in stranger-storm-art/). Two versions, Eric picks:

A, painterly (round 1's art, unchanged: "keep the ice as is")
  A-painterly-phone-fire-turn.gif / .mp4       phone, light, the storm's 2nd turn (fire leads)
  A-painterly-computer-ice-turn.gif / .mp4     computer, LIGHT this time, the storm's 1st turn (ice leads)
  A-painterly-phone-three-turns.png            the storm's three turns side by side: the crack whole, 2/3, 1/3
  A-painterly-computer-fire-turn.png           computer, light, a fire turn, full screen
B, the GBA pass (the same storm and motion, redone as pixels)
  B-gba-phone-fire-turn.gif / .mp4, B-gba-computer-ice-turn.gif / .mp4, B-gba-phone-three-turns.png, B-gba-computer-fire-turn.png
The MP4s and PNGs show the true pixels and colors; the GIFs are 256 colors and scaled down (B's pixels look softer in them).

What changed for Eric's notes
- The crack stays (17:25): the opening's own cracks (fire-side lava-orange, ice-side blue, a dark fissure with a hot core), on the
  board under the pieces through the storm's three turns, each branch shorter from its tip every turn: whole, 2/3, about 1/3, then
  gone with the storm. (Mocked by putting the scene's real cracks back in the live page; A soft-edged, B hard-edged.)
- See-through, not dark (17:30): the storm itself at 88% over everything it overlaps (Moves, his figure, the strip all still show
  through); a soft dim round the board, 22% just off it and a little more toward the far edges and corners; none on the squares.
  The computer shots are light mode now (round 1's computer video was the game's dark mode, which is likely what read as "too dark").
- The overlap stays (17:25): nothing held back at the top or sides. Only the bottom stays low, so the line under the board and the
  clock read.
- B, the GBA pass (17:40): 250 px frames scaled up as hard pixels (about 1.7 px a pixel on a phone, 3.8 on a computer: his figure's
  own pixel size), 10 colors each for fire and ice (15-bit), ordered dithering between them, three hard see-through levels for the
  glow, whole frames at 12 a second (no cross-fade). The detail is kept: the same tongues, embers, ribbons, shards and icicles.

What would ship
- A: storm.webp 1029 KB + frost.webp 34 KB (30 frames, 400 px; about 26 MB decoded), neighbors cross-faded on a 400 px canvas.
- B: storm.webp 376 KB + frost.webp 1 KB (36 frames, 250 px, lossless; about 12 MB decoded), whole frames on a 250 px canvas
  shown as pixels (like today's ring). B is the lighter one to load and to run.
- Either way: 2 to 6 image draws a frame, no particles. The dim is one still CSS layer; the crack one still SVG a turn.

Calls for the director / Eric
1. A or B (or B's pixels a notch finer or coarser: it's one number).
2. The dim's strength (22%) and the storm's see-through (88%): both are single numbers, easy to move after a playtest.
3. The crack keeps the opening's shape (two bursts where the swords went in). One long crack across the board would be new art.
